---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="Learn about Kay Breaux Livaudais, abstract painter">
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-20 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 md:gap-16 items-start">
        <!-- Photo placeholder -->
        <div class="aspect-[3/4] bg-warm-200 rounded-sm flex items-center justify-center">
          <p class="text-warm-400 font-body italic">Artist photo</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Bio content -->
        <div>
          <h1 class="font-heading text-[21px] text-warm-700 mb-8 uppercase tracking-wide-3 font-normal">About the Artist</h1>

          <div class="space-y-6 text-warm-400 text-[15px] leading-[28px]">
            <p>
              Kay Breaux Livaudais has always been a maker. She has roots in southwestern Louisiana and grew up in New Orleans. An attitude of independence and passion for art led Kay to pursue studio art in college in Massachusetts and Paris. Architecture school followed as an avenue to merge her artistic skills and sensibilities with the desire to design space at human scale. Kay raised a family, retired from her career as an architect and found her way back to the studio, painting and working in clay, about fifteen years ago. Curiosity enlivens her practice and working sessions with fellow artists continue to refine and expand her skills. Her confidence in drawing, love of color and understanding of spatial structure are evident in her energetic compositions.
            </p>
            <p>
              Kay says painting often feels like she is standing on her head. It is disorienting, dizzying, wobbly, or in and out of her control. She is always listening, observing, and keen to embrace a serendipitous source of inspiration. Her work is shaped by memories, daily rhythms, gratitude, emotion, the sultry landscape of Louisiana and the urban grit of New Orleans. She finds joy, frustration, and indeed, many emotions in the give and take of developing a painting intuitively. Creating layers of luscious color, movement and texture, she relies on her hands as much as her brushes to tell a story. Pursuing an end to the tale, she takes the time to wonder, to think and to not think.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Artist Statement -->
          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-warm-200">
            <h2 class="font-heading text-[21px] text-warm-700 mb-4 uppercase tracking-wide-3 font-normal">Artist Statement</h2>
            <div class="text-warm-400 italic text-[15px] leading-[28px] space-y-6">
              <p>
                "What is the seed of inspiration for my work? It is love, loss, daily events, memories, an image, a fleeting thought, a velvety gardenia's divine fragrance and humid days in the south. Intent is often playing hide and seek with me. Frustration, elation, patience and taking the time to daydream and ponder fuel the progress of my work.
              </p>
              <p>
                I often wonder how to express a deeply felt emotion or even a daily ritual. Threads of nostalgia and a deep sense of gratitude are woven throughout my practice. How shall I make the gestures and marks to define these abstract ideas, or a deeply felt memory that has seeped into my body? I ask myself about the scale of pieces and parts, the color palette, and how the tactile quality of my materials will enrich my project. Curiosity and desire keep me moving forward."
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
